<article class="env-remark" id="072Z">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/072Z">Remark <span data-tag="072Z">9.7.8.22</span> <span class="named">(Ordering the Idempotents)</span>.</a> Let $\mathbb {K}$ be a collection of simplicial sets and let </p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  Q = \{  \textnormal{ $\mathbb {K}$-cocomplete idempotents $\operatorname{\mathcal{E}}$} \}  / \textnormal{Equivalence} \]
</div>
<p> be the collection of equivalence classes of $\mathbb {K}$-cocomplete idempotent $\infty $-categories. If $\operatorname{\mathcal{E}}$ is a $\mathbb {K}$-cocomplete idempotent, we write $[ \operatorname{\mathcal{E}}]$ for its equivalence class (regarded as an element of $Q$). Then we can endow $Q$ with a partial ordering $\leq $, where we write $[ \operatorname{\mathcal{E}}] \leq [ \operatorname{\mathcal{E}}' ]$ if every $\mathbb {K}$-cocomplete $\operatorname{\mathcal{E}}$-module is also a $\mathbb {K}$-cocomplete $\operatorname{\mathcal{E}}'$-module. This relation is evidently reflexive and transitive. It is also antisymmetric: that is, if $\operatorname{\mathcal{E}}$ and $\operatorname{\mathcal{E}}'$ are $\mathbb {K}$-cocomplete idempotents such that $\operatorname{\mathcal{E}}$ is a $\mathbb {K}$-cocomplete $\operatorname{\mathcal{E}}'$-module and $\operatorname{\mathcal{E}}'$ is a $\mathbb {K}$-cocomplete $\operatorname{\mathcal{E}}$-module, then $\operatorname{\mathcal{E}}$ and $\operatorname{\mathcal{E}}'$ are equivalent. To prove this, fix objects $E \in \operatorname{\mathcal{E}}$ and $E' \in \operatorname{\mathcal{E}}'$ which exhibit $\operatorname{\mathcal{E}}$ and $\operatorname{\mathcal{E}}'$ as $\mathbb {K}$-cocomplete idempotents. Then Proposition <a data-tag="072T" href="/tag/072T">9.7.8.16</a> guarantees that the functors </p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \operatorname{\mathcal{E}}\xrightarrow { \otimes E'} \operatorname{\mathcal{E}}\otimes _{ \mathbb {K} } \operatorname{\mathcal{E}}' \xleftarrow { E \otimes } \operatorname{\mathcal{E}}' \]
</div>
<p> are equivalences of $\infty $-categories, so that $\operatorname{\mathcal{E}}$ and $\operatorname{\mathcal{E}}'$ are equivalent to one another. </p>
</article>
